  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and well above normal conditions continue today.\n\nSurface high pressure centered off the coast of northern Maine this \nmorning, will continue to progress northeast into the northern \nAtlantic today. Meanwhile, the next mid-level trough will be \napproaching the region. While dry weather will persist today, the \napproaching trough, will support upper level cloud cover today. \nAdditionally 850mb temperatures will be similar to the day prior, \nsupporting highs in the low to mid 60s today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler air arrives Monday, supporting chances for \nsome light rain and/or snow early in the week.\n\nThe mid to upper level pattern late this weekend and into the \nearliest portions of next week will feature a trough overspreading \nthe region from the north-central United States Sunday to the \neastern Great Lakes through Tuesday, where a ridge will then build \neast Tuesday night through Wednesday. Meanwhile at the surface, a \ncold front will sweep east across western and north-central New York \nSunday night, with surface high pressure gradually shifting east \nacross north-central United States and into the Great Lakes Monday \nthrough Wednesday.\n\nGetting more into the specifics, with the frontal passage Sunday \nnight into Monday morning, expect scattered rain showers to \noverspread the region from west to east due to low-level convergence \nand moist ascent along the cold front and moist isentropic lift \nahead of the trough axis aloft. Overall, precipitation amounts will \nbe very light.\n\nIn the wake of the frontal passage, a colder airmass will filter \nacross the region Monday. While a dry weather is expected to return, \nthe post-front airmass should be cold enough to support lake effect \nrain and/or snow showers southeast and south of the eastern Great \nLakes Monday into Tuesday. Best timing for lake effect percipition \nwill be Monday night due to an uptick in low-level moisture. Any \nlake effect precipitation should end by Tuesday morning as a \nlowering subsidence inversion and continued low-level dry air \nadvection cause lake-induced instability to wane.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Seasonable temperatures persist throughout the week, \nwith the next chance for some cool rain to arrive Thursday.\n\nAttention then turns to the later half of the week as the next \npotent mid-level trough dive southeast across the central portions \nof the CONUS. During it's dive across the Central Plains Wednesday, \na shortwave trough will round the base of the trough over the Gulf \nStates. Meanwhile, a coastal low will develop off the shores of the \nCarolinas and proceed northeastward along the Atlantic Seaboard \ntoward the Northeast. Given this system remains multiple days out, \nthere continues to remain discrepancies with its track and \ndevelopment.\n\nAs of this forecast iteration, forecast trends are suggesting a cold \nrain for the Thanksgiving holiday with minimal to next to no impacts \nto travel across Western and North Central New York.\n\nStay tuned for how this system develops and tracks heading into the \nholiday weekend.",
